              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesRelationBetweenAQFTAndFQFT"} Relation between algebraic and functorial field theory
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} Dynamics
            affects both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            and,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+space+and+quantity"} dually
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            but applies equally well to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In the
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong Heisenberg picture
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
                (or more generally
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag%E2%80%93Kastler+axioms"} Haag–Kastler axioms
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %li
              %p
                In the
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong Schrödinger picture
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagator"} propagated
                through
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+category"} cobordism category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+%28interaction%29+picture"} Dirac (interaction) picture
                is a mixture of these two approaches: dynamics is split into a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
                (or otherwise
                = succeed ")" do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    solvable
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/solvable+system"} ?
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} interaction or perturbation
          %p
            The pictures are named after those physicists (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Werner+Heisenberg"}> Werner Heisenberg
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erwin+Schr%C3%B6dinger"} Erwin Schrödinger
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Dirac"} Paul Dirac
          %h2#formulation Formulation
          %h3#with_global_time With global time
          %p
            Let us assume a global notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} fixed background
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            which is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetime"} globally hyperbolic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surfaces"} Cauchy surfaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate"} coordinate
            for this foliation. The upshot of this is that each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/event"} event
            occurs at a time
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            at any time
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            (at least within certain bounds). Thus we may speak sensibly of either the state of the world at time
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            or the value of some observable quantity at time
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          %p
            Because this is a picture of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            %span.newWikiWord
              time evolution
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/time+evolution"} ?
            operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(t,t')
            between any two times
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t,t'
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(t) \coloneqq U(t,0)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ′
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(t,t') = U(t) \circ U(t')^{-1}
          %p
            In the Heisenberg picture, for each observable
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            only at some time
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(t)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(t) = A(0) \cdot U(t)
          %p
            to show the evolution of the observable through time. However, when it comes to the state of the world, we speak of a single state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            that describes the world at all times.
          %p
            In the Schrödinger picture, we instead speak of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi(t)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo *
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi(t) = U(t) \ast \psi(0)
          %p
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            across all times.
          %p
            To see the connection between the two pictures, recall that an observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and a state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            together produce a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+distribution"} probability distribution
            giving the probability that any given value of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            will be observed, given that the world is in state
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+states"} pure states
            are all
            = succeed "s.)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+measure"} delta measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            belongs to an appropriate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            and the probability measures are sufficiently nice, we may restrict attention to the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle{A}\rangle_\psi
            of these distributions, since the entire distribution can be recovered from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle{A^n}\rangle_\psi
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            varies over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
          %p The connection between the two pictures is then given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                      %mi ψ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle{A \cdot U(t)}\rangle_\psi = \langle{A}\rangle_{U(t) \ast \psi} .
          %p
            It remains to say exactly what
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(t)
            is and what the operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            are. Let us use the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrix"} density matrix
            formulation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+statistical+mechanics"} quantum statistical mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle{A}\rangle_\psi = \tr(A \psi)
          %p
            (using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            operation). Each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(t)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary operator
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            (since time evolution between Cauchy surfaces is a symmetry), and we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi A
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cdot U(t) = U(t)^{-1} A U(t)
          %p
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+action"} right action
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
